  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1213 PM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThe pattern turns considerably more tranquil into Tuesday with\nlighter and drier northwest flow aloft prevailing. Skies will turn\nclear through the late morning with batches of high cirrus filling\nin later in the day. Surface breezes will be much more subdued, \nand temperatures will run just a couple to a few degrees below \nearly November climatology.  \n\nWe continue to assess model solutions and their depiction of a\nclosed low that moves inland off of the Pacific and crosses the\nGreat Basin into the central Rockies by Wednesday. This feature\ndoes look to host some baroclinicity with its 90-100 kt jet streak\nrounding its base, but overall precipitation looks to remain\nconfined within the core of the low due to weaker and\nunestablished dynamics aloft. This would keep most of the\nprecipitation in UT and CO, with just isolated to scattered POPs\nfor our northern NM zones through Wednesday night. Guidance does \nnot advertise very strong winds for Wednesday, but this will need \nto be monitored, as upper level momentum will be increasing.\n\nSlightly cooler air would filter into NM on the backside of this\ndeparting low by Thursday. Daytime temperatures would still run\nnear to just slightly below normal on Thursday with light to\nmoderate breezes prevailing. \n\nDry west northwest flow looks to prevail into Friday and Saturday\nwith a weak perturbation meandering nearby, but posing no threat \nfor precipitation. Another wave of cooler air will filter in on \nSaturday as another low drops into the central plains, but again \ndaytime highs will stay near to only slightly below normal. A \nbuilding ridge early next week would suggest slightly warmer \nreadings with tranquility persisting.",
